---

<ChapterLayout
  title="6.1 — Tree of Life · AST 100"
  description="Every living thing on one tree, rooted at a single ancestral cell — and the surprise that bacteria hold nearly all of life's diversity while animals, plants, and fungi are one thin twig."
  activeId="6.1" chapter={6} current="/chapter/6/6.1">
  <Hero
    tease="Everything alive is related. Not poetically — literally, by descent, from a single cell that lived four billion years ago. We can prove it, because every organism on Earth still uses the same four-letter alphabet and the same genetic dictionary. And when you draw the family tree honestly, the result is humbling: the entire visible living world, every animal and plant you have ever seen, is one thin twig on a bush of microbes."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="tree">One Tree, One Ancestor</h2>
      <p>
        The Chemical Age ended with a single cell (<a href={subPath("5.3")}>§5.3</a>). The Biological Age is what that
        cell did next — and the headline is easy to state and hard to absorb: <strong>it is still here</strong>. Not the
        cell itself, but its lineage, unbroken, in every living thing. You are not descended from a common ancestor in
        some vague, metaphorical sense. You are a physical continuation of a cell that lived on a young, airless planet
        roughly four billion years ago, and so is every bacterium in your gut, every tree outside, and every fish in the
        sea.
      </p>
      <p>
        How can we possibly know that? Because life is full of arbitrary choices that never changed. There is no
        chemical necessity for genetic information to be written in <em>four</em> letters — A, T, C, and G — yet every
        organism ever sequenced uses exactly those four. There is no necessity for the three-letter word "GGA" to mean
        the amino acid glycine — yet it does, in bacteria, in oak trees, and in you. Shared arbitrary choices are the
        fingerprint of shared ancestry: they are how you would know two students copied the same essay. Step through
        the four universal features in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="What every living thing shares">
        <UniversalPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        With those shared features, biologists can do something remarkable: measure how <em>closely</em> two organisms
        are related, by counting how much their shared molecules have drifted apart. Compare the same ancient protein
        across thousands of species, tally the differences, and the pattern of similarities reconstructs the family
        tree — not guessed from what things look like, but computed from what they are made of. Recent trees are built
        from DNA read straight out of soil, seawater, and mud, from organisms nobody has ever managed to grow in a
        laboratory. Explore the result below — click any branch, or walk them with the arrow keys.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The tree of life">
        <TreePanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <h2 id="domains">Three Domains — and a Humbling</h2>
      <p>
        Life sorts into three great <strong>domains</strong>. The <strong>Bacteria</strong> you have heard of. The
        <strong>Archaea</strong> look like bacteria under a microscope but are built differently underneath —
        different membranes, different machinery — and many of them live in places that would kill anything else:
        boiling springs, salt lakes, acid, the rock kilometres beneath your feet. And the <strong>Eukarya</strong>: the
        organisms whose cells keep their DNA in a nucleus. That is us, along with every animal, plant, fungus, and
        amoeba.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now for the humbling. Look at the proportions of that tree again. Almost all of life's genetic diversity sits in
        the bacteria. An enormous slab of it — the <strong>Candidate Phyla Radiation</strong> — consists of microbes so
        small and so dependent on other cells that essentially none of them have ever been grown in a laboratory; we
        know they exist only because we can now read DNA directly out of the environment. And <em>everything you have
        ever seen with your eyes</em> — every animal, every plant, every mushroom, the entire subject matter of
        natural history — occupies one narrow sliver of one branch. Life on Earth is, and always has been, a microbial
        affair. We are a rounding error that learned to look at the tree.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one more twist, and it is the setup for the next lesson. Notice <em>where</em> the eukaryotes attach.
        They are not a third trunk rising independently from the root. On the best current evidence, they grew out from
        <strong>within the archaea</strong> — which means that you, at the deepest level of your cellular ancestry, are
        a kind of archaeon. A kind of archaeon that, at some point, swallowed a bacterium and did not digest it.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>One alphabet, one dictionary, one ancestor — and a tree whose proportions put us firmly in our place. But
        the branch we sit on was made by an accident so strange it deserves a lesson of its own.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <PrevNext />
</ChapterLayout>
